Co-located mode: a directory that holds no `.hll` files of its own is
walked recursively, looking for service directories — a directory with
exactly one `.hll` file that declares a `service`. Each one builds in
place, next to its own source, with no `--out` needed. Intermediate
directories that hold no service, `homelab/services/` here, are walked
through rather than treated as an error.

```console
$ hllc --build homelab
homelab/services/web/docker-compose.yml

```

As in flat mode, a tree with no service anywhere in it exits 0 and
says what it looked for, rather than leaving the user to wonder why
nothing appeared. Co-located mode keeps its own `built` counter, so
this pairs with the block above for the same reason
`build-directory.trycmd`'s two blocks do: each direction catches a
different way of breaking the counter.

```console
$ hllc --build library
library: no service directories found (looked for a single .hll file declaring a `service`, in this directory or any subdirectory) — nothing was built

```
